00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:25.230
A user posted an article called Trump has
a Point about the polls from Politico.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:33.200
And the gist of this article is that there
were some state polls in 2016 that were wrong

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.290
and there were some state polls in 2016 that
said Hillary Clinton was going to win by a

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.480
small margin and Trump won by a small margin.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.480
And certainly that's true.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:49.060
And, you know, my my goal on this show has
been not to inaccurately assail polling more

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:55.750
than it deserves, but also not to hold polling
up as any kind of immutable and unchanging

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.750
metric that tells us exactly what's going
to happen in November, in 2016.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.160
The polls were widely accurate.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.260
The national polls said Hillary Clinton was
going to win by three and she won by a little

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.720
more than two.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.060
That that means they were right.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.060
They were.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.290
The results were within the margin of error
in most states.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.430
They were right.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:21.650
It is true that when states are really, really
close, sometimes state polling that is done

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:28.810
even five days before the election may not
reflect who is going to win that state.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.270
And certainly Michigan, Wisconsin and some
other states are examples of that.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.220
So what we should take away from this is not
that polling is widely untrustworthy when

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.820
we are talking about high, highly rated pollsters.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.220
It's understanding that there is a margin
of error and that things can change.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.210
Also in the last few days before an election.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:55.659
So if it tells us anything, it's that no matter
what state you're voting in, you need to vote.

00:01:55.659 --> 00:01:58.630
And that no matter what the polls say, you
need to vote.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:01:59.800
That's the message.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:07.250
And there's no you know, there's no big revelation
about the the particularly inaccurate polling

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.310
in 2016.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.629
The polls were mostly right nationally and
at the state level.

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:19.999
The polls did not account for some momentum
and swings in Trump's direction close to the

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:22.230
end of the campaign.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.560
And there were states where Hillary was leading
in the polls and she ultimately lost.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.629
What that tells us is the polls give us a
sense of what's going on, but we need to absolutely

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:36.569
crush turnout to ensure that Trump doesn't
end up winning some of these states by 11000

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:38.549
votes, again, like he did in Michigan.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:39.860
That's the takeaway.

